-    {
-      "id": "US-832",
-      "title": "PDPA/GDPR data portability endpoint: export all personal data by JID",
-      "priority": "high",
-      "description": "Malaysia's amended PDPA (effective June 2025) grants data subjects the right to request their personal data in a portable, machine-readable format. The existing GDPR erasure endpoint handles deletion, but there is no corresponding data export endpoint. Businesses processing data from over 20,000 contacts are now legally obligated to fulfil portability requests.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "New endpoint `GET /api/rainbow/guests/:jid/data-export` returns all personal data for the JID as structured JSON",
-        "Export includes: all messages (content, timestamps, direction), conversation metadata, intent predictions, feedback records, opt-out status, and conversation state",
-        "Response includes a `generated_at` ISO timestamp and `retention_policy` summary",
-        "Request is logged to `gdpr_audit_log` table with `action: 'data_export'`",
-        "Endpoint requires admin authentication (same auth as erasure endpoint)",
-        "Response time under 5 seconds for up to 10,000 messages; returns 202 with a job ID for larger datasets"
-      ],
-      "technicalNotes": [
-        "Mirror the query pattern from `src/routes/admin/gdpr-erasure.ts` but SELECT instead of DELETE",
-        "Canonicalize the JID using `canonicalPhoneKey()` before querying — same as erasure endpoint",
-        "Use `Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=data-export-{hash}.json` header for download"
-      ],
-      "dependencies": [],
-      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
-      "passes": true
-    },
-    {
-      "id": "US-833",
-      "title": "Per-JID inbound message rate limiter to prevent bot abuse",
-      "priority": "high",
-      "description": "A single WhatsApp number can flood the bot with hundreds of messages per minute, exhausting AI provider quotas and degrading service for all users. The existing rate limiter operates at a profile level, not per individual user JID. A sliding-window per-JID limiter should throttle abusive senders with a polite rejection message.",
-      "acceptanceCriteria": [
-        "Each unique sender JID is limited to 10 messages per 60-second sliding window (configurable per profile in settings.json)",
-        "When the limit is exceeded, the bot sends one brief reply: 'Too many messages — please wait a moment before sending more.' and drops subsequent messages until the window resets",
-        "The throttle reply is sent at most once per window (not once per dropped message)",
-        "Rate limit counters are in-memory (not Redis-dependent) with TTL-based cleanup to avoid memory leaks",
-        "Admin dashboard shows current throttled JID count and throttle events in the last 24 hours"
-      ],
-      "technicalNotes": [
-        "Implement as middleware in `src/assistant/message-router.ts` before pipeline dispatch",
-        "Use a `Map<string, {count: number, windowStart: number}>` with periodic cleanup (every 5 minutes)",
-        "Default limits: 10 msg/60s; configurable `rateLimiting.perUserWindowMs` and `perUserMaxMessages` in settings.json",
-        "Distinct from `src/lib/frequency-cap.ts` (outbound cap) — this is inbound abuse prevention"
-      ],
-      "dependencies": [],
-      "estimatedComplexity": "small",
-      "passes": true
